  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "            Unit 3's notes explain how Spanish conveys concepts like 'to like', purified water, clitic pronouns, location, and obligation. \nTo express liking something, Spanish uses the verb 'gustar' with a pronoun indicating the person to whom something is pleasing—for example, 'Me gusta el café' means 'I like coffee' but literally 'Coffee is pleasing to me'. \nThe term 'agua mineral' varies by region and can refer to carbonated water or purified drinking water, sometimes called by a brand name. \nClitic pronouns (like 'me' and 'la' in 'súbamela') attach to verbs and will be treated in later units. \n'Cerca' means 'near' but when followed by a noun, Spanish requires the preposition 'de': 'cerca de la casa' ('near the house'). \nFinally, the idiom 'hay que' conveys obligation, similar to 'it's necessary to' or 'one should'.\n"
    }
  ],
